{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_awbwnsfhulxzpkgzdprnyaxqxd2x26i7wnadi3luwyrcctvudk3q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Clean build, byte-identical output.\n\n## What the C program does\n`scanf(\"%lf\", &f)` (leaving `f == 0.0` on failure), then `printf(\"%llx %a %.4f\\n\", raw_bits, f, f)`.\n\n## Artifacts (verified on disk)\n- `Cargo.toml` — bin target `driver`\n- `src/main.rs` — 578 lines, builds with **zero warnings**\n\n## Behaviors reproduced (all validated empirically against the compiled C binary)\n1. **glibc `scanf(\"%lf\")` token collection**, including its quirks discovered by differential testing:\n   - `0x` / `-0x` alone → *matching failure* (`f` stays `+0.0`, so `-0x` prints **positive** zero), while `-0x.` → `-0.0`\n   - `-0xp3` fails because glibc requires `got_digit` before accepting the exponent char (my first version wrongly used \"buffer non-empty\" — caught by testing)\n   - `1e`, `1e+`, `0x1p` → longest-valid-prefix (`1.0`); `nan(123)` → plain `0x7ff8...` (scanf, unlike `strtod`, drops the payload); `infi`/`infin` → failure; `inf`/`infinity` case-insensitive; `_` and `e` in hex handled per glibc\n2. **strtod value determination**: decimal via Rust's correctly-rounded parser (canonicalized, exponent-length guarded); hex floats implemented manually with round-half-to-even, sticky bits, subnormal/overflow handling\n3. **`%a`** exactly as glibc's `printf_fphex`: leading `0` digit for subnormals with `p-1022`, `0x0p+0` for zero, trailing-zero trimming, dot omission, always-signed exponent, `-nan`/`-inf`\n4. **`%.4f`**: `nan`/`-nan`/`inf`/`-inf` special-cased (Rust prints `NaN`), otherwise `{:.4}` which matches glibc's round-half-to-even and full exact expansions (300+ digit values verified)\n5. **Streaming stdin**: byte-at-a-time reads, so it returns immediately on `\"2.5 \"` without waiting for EOF — same as C\n\n## Test evidence\n- 154 hand-built edge cases: **0 diffs*